'''Akyatan''', officially the '''City of Akyatan''' ([[Dagiti language|Dagiti]]: ''Dakbanwa sang Akyatan'' or ''Lungsod ng Akyatan''), is a [[highly urbanized city]] and the capital of the [[Dagit|Republic of Dagit]]. Situated on the eastern coast of the main island of Kabisayaan, Akyatan serves as the nation's political, economic, and cultural center. With a population of 3.8 million as of 2024, it is the largest city in Dagit and one of the most populous metropolitan areas in the [[Shendan Ocean]] region.

The city is built upon the historic site of '''Cinc Parets''' (Five Walls), a massive fortified trading warehouse complex constructed by [[Volisania|Volisanian]] explorer [[Ernesto Lacunya]] in 1605. Unlike typical Illypnian colonial settlements, Cinc Parets was established not as a military conquest but as a neutral commercial hub where Dagiti merchants, Volisanian traders, and representatives from across the Shendan Ocean could conduct business. The name "Akyatan" derives from the Dagiti word meaning "climbing" or "place of scaling," officially adopted in 1850 to commemorate the pivotal moment during the [[Dagiti Revolution]] when revolutionary forces scaled the walls to overthrow the merchant oligarchy that had seized control of the complex.

Akyatan is organized into '''seven geographical districts''': Ciudad Vieja (Old City), Puerto Lacunya, San Esteban, Dagumnan, Kawayanan, Sampaguita, and Isla Verde. Each district maintains its distinct character, from the colonial heritage architecture of Ciudad Vieja to the gleaming skyscrapers of the modern financial district in Puerto Lacunya.

== Etymology ==
The name "Akyatan" translates directly to "climbing" or "the place of scaling" in the [[Dagiti language]]. This name was officially adopted on 1 June 1850 following the successful Dagiti Revolution, serving as a direct, literal symbol of the nation's liberation from merchant oligarchy rule. It commemorates the night of 21 April 1850, when revolutionary ''timawa'' fighters scaled the five massive walls of Cinc Parets under cover of a torrential typhoon, seizing the walled commercial district from the merchant aristocracy.

Prior to 1850, the city was known as '''Cinc Parets''' (Five Walls), named for the fortified trading warehouse complex established by Ernesto Lacunya. The original Dagiti settlement in the area was called '''Dakbayan''' (meaning "the city" or "the place by the bay"), which served as one of the major trading centers of the [[Kingdom of Maputik]] under the traditional ''kadatuan'' nobility.

== History ==
=== Pre-colonial era ===
{{main|Kingdom of Maputik}}

[[File:Dagiti Karakoa Fleet.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A fleet of traditional Dagiti ''karakoa'' warships patrolling the waters near Dakbayan in the late 16th century.]]

Prior to Volisanian contact, the site of present-day Akyatan was occupied by '''Dakbayan''', a major trading settlement within the [[Kingdom of Maputik]]. The settlement was established at the fertile estuarine mouth of what is now the Dagiti River, where silty waters created ideal conditions for both rice cultivation and maritime trade.

By the late 16th century, Dakbayan had evolved into a cosmopolitan trading center under the reign of '''Datu Pula''', known historically as the "Red Chief" for the extensive crimson [[Visayan]]-style tattoos covering his face and torso. Following the [[Battle of Suba ng Daet]] in 1588, he centralized control of the harbor, instituted a harsh tribute system, and constructed a massive fleet of outrigger warships called ''[[karakoa]]s''. During the pivotal [[Battle of Sugbu Strait]] in 1595, his forces successfully ambushed a major Zongese tributary convoy, forcing the mainland empire to formally recognize Dakbayan's monopoly over the eastern spice trade.

However, this period also saw the rise of the '''Secret Trade'''—a devastating system of slave raids conducted by pirates and mercenaries operating in the employ of Zongese planters in the New World colony of [[Sinchew]]. Between the 1570s and 1750s, an estimated 15,000–20,000 Dagiti were seized and transported to Sinchew. The crisis contributed directly to Datu Pula's rise to power, as his ''karakoa'' fleet was designed to intercept and destroy the slave raiders.

=== Establishment of Cinc Parets ===
{{main|Ernesto Lacunya|Cinc Parets}}

In 1605, the geopolitical landscape of [[Adonia]] was permanently altered when the [[Volisanian people|Volisanian]] explorer and navigator [[Ernesto Lacunya]] arrived in the archipelago. Tasked with establishing a strategic counter-weight to Fosian colonial expansion following the [[Great Disappointment]], Lacunya bypassed established Zongese territories and aimed his expedition toward the Kingdom of Maputik.

Unlike the aggressive colonization efforts favored by Fosian powers, Lacunya pursued a fundamentally different approach. Recognizing Dagit's immense strategic value as a neutral trading midpoint, he established peaceful relations with local leaders and constructed '''Cinc Parets''' (Five Walls)—a massive, fortified trading warehouse complex that would serve as the cornerstone of Volisanian commerce in the region. The five walls were designed not as military fortifications, but as secure enclosures for valuable goods awaiting trade.

Rather than occupying the islands, Lacunya invited Dagiti merchants, Volisanian traders, and eventually representatives from the [[Kingdom of Fenia]], the [[Kingdom of Zong]], [[Mitoyagi]] ([[Okaiken]]), [[Nakamizu]], and [[Yanbaru]] to conduct business within the walls of Cinc Parets. Under his fair administration, the settlement quickly grew into a bustling, cosmopolitan hub for trans-oceanic trade. Dagit remained an independent territory with its own governance under the Kingdom of Maputik; Lacunya's presence was purely commercial.

=== The Cinc Parets–Macaio Galleon Trade ===
{{main|Cinc Parets–Macaio Galleon Trade}}

[[File:Cinc Parets Trading Hub.jpg|thumb|left|250px|An 18th-century illustration of Cinc Parets, showing the five walled enclosures and the bustling commercial activity within.]]

Following Lacunya's establishment of Cinc Parets, the trading hub became the eastern terminus of one of the most lucrative trade routes in Adonian history: the [[Cinc Parets–Macaio Galleon Trade]]. This trans-oceanic circuit connected the Shendan Ocean to the [[Algarbes]] archipelago in the western hemisphere ([[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]]), facilitating the exchange of Illypnian manufactured goods, Fosian silks and ceramics, and New World silver.

Volisanian galleons departed from the port of [[Macaio]] in [[Balisca]], carrying New World silver and exotic goods. These vessels arrived at Cinc Parets, where the silver was exchanged for Shendan Ocean commodities: spices from Dagit, porcelain and silk from [[Zong]], pearls from [[Yanbaru]], and lacquerware from [[Okaiken|Mitoyagi]]. This arrangement made Dagit the indispensable intermediary in the Illypnian–Fosian trade circuit.

The galleon trade also facilitated the movement of people. Volisanian merchants established permanent families in Cinc Parets, intermarrying with Dagiti women and creating a distinct ''mestizo'' class that spoke both Dagiti and Volisanian. These merchant families became the backbone of the trade network, serving as translators, cultural brokers, and commercial agents. Over generations, Volisanian loanwords entered the Dagiti lexicon, creating a diglossic linguistic environment that persists to this day.

=== The Merchant Aristocracy ===

By the early 19th century, the social order of the Kingdom of Maputik had undergone a profound transformation. The centuries of the galleon trade had generated immense wealth, but this wealth became increasingly concentrated in the hands of a small merchant aristocracy. The great trading families—many of mixed Dagiti-Volisanian or Dagiti-Zongese descent—had parlayed their commercial success into political power, gradually eclipsing the traditional authority of the ''kadatuan'' nobility.

The process of aristocratic consolidation began in earnest during the reign of Datu Malubos (1785–1812), who granted the merchant families unprecedented privileges in exchange for financial support. By 1800, the merchant aristocracy had effectively transformed Cinc Parets into an autonomous zone within the Kingdom. The original five walls—constructed by Ernesto Lacunya as secure enclosures for trade goods—had been expanded and fortified, creating a walled city-within-a-city.

The situation deteriorated further following the death of Datu Malubos in 1812. His successor, Datu Kalbo, was a minor who ruled under a regency dominated by the merchant families. During this period, the merchant aristocracy systematically dismantled the remaining traditional institutions that had protected the common people. The ''timawa'', once a proud warrior class, were reduced to debt peons. The ''oripun'', whose status had gradually improved under the Kaharian system, found themselves pushed back into harsh conditions of debt bondage.

The final blow came in 1835, when the merchant families orchestrated the deposition of Datu Kalbo and the installation of a puppet ruler, Datu Lusaw. Under his reign, the Kingdom of Maputik existed in name only; real power was exercised by the ''Conseho sang mga Negosyante''—the Council of Merchants—that governed Cinc Parets as an independent oligarchy.

=== The Dagiti Revolution of 1850 ===
{{main|Dagiti Revolution of 1850}}

[[File:Scaling of Cinc Parets.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A 19th-century artistic rendering of revolutionary ''timawa'' fighters scaling the walls of Cinc Parets during the April 1850 assault.]]

The revolution began on the night of 15 March 1850, when coordinated uprisings erupted across the archipelago under the leadership of the ''Katipunan sang Kagawasan'' (Society of Freedom), led by [[Mateu Malvar]]. The merchant aristocracy, caught off guard by the scale and coordination of the uprising, attempted to rally their private security forces and call upon foreign allies for assistance.

The decisive moment came in the assault on Cinc Parets itself. The walled commercial district had been transformed by the merchant aristocracy into a formidable fortress, with expanded walls, private garrisons, and artillery positions. The revolutionaries, lacking heavy weapons, turned to the tactics of their ancestors—the lightning-fast amphibious raids that had once made the Dagiti sea-lords the terror of the Jade Sea.

On the night of 21 April 1850, under the cover of a torrential typhoon, the revolutionary forces launched their assault on Cinc Parets. Teams of ''timawa'' fighters—trained in traditional techniques of coastal raiding—scaled the five walls using tactics refined over centuries of maritime warfare. By dawn, the revolutionaries had seized key positions within the walls, and the merchant garrisons found themselves surrounded.

The merchant families, realizing their position was untenable, surrendered unconditionally. The Council of Merchants was dissolved, the private armies were disbanded, and the assets of the trading houses were seized for the new Republic.

=== Renaming to Akyatan ===

In the weeks following the capture of Cinc Parets, the revolutionary council deliberated on the fate of the walled commercial district. The decision was made to preserve the walls but to transform their meaning. On 1 June 1850, the revolutionary council formally proclaimed that Cinc Parets would be renamed '''Akyatan'''—commemorating the revolutionary assault and symbolizing the broader ascent of the common people from oppression to freedom.

The renaming ceremony was attended by thousands of revolutionary fighters, traditional nobles, religious leaders, and ordinary citizens. Mateu Malvar, acclaimed as the ''Ama sang Republika'' (Father of the Republic), delivered an address articulating the vision of the new Dagit. The name Akyatan, he declared, would forever remind the Dagiti people that their freedom had been won not through the favor of foreign powers or the benevolence of elites, but through their own courage and sacrifice.

=== The Iron Envoys and the opening of Mitoyagi ===
{{main|Iron Envoys|Convention of Iwaki}}

[[File:Iron Envoys 1853.png|thumb|left|300px|Admiral Vicente Dasmariñas's "Iron Envoys" steam into Okami Bay on July 8, 1853, bringing working models of telegraphs, steam locomotives, and textile looms.]]

The establishment of the Republic of Dagit transformed the archipelago from a commercial entrepôt into a sovereign maritime power. The new Republic inherited the wealth of the galleon trade and the sophisticated naval infrastructure of the Kingdom of Maputik. Under President Mateu Malvar, Dagit began to project its power beyond the Shendan Ocean.

On July 8, 1853, a squadron of four steam-powered frigates commanded by Admiral [[Vicente Dasmariñas]] steamed into the bay of [[Okami]] in [[Mitoyagi]] (modern [[Okaiken]]). The vessels, painted black and belching smoke, presented a spectacle unlike anything the Sanese had witnessed. The [[Convention of Iwaki]] (1854) and subsequent [[Treaty of Amity and Commerce (1858)|Treaty of Amity and Commerce]] opened Mitoyagi to Dagiti and Zong merchants, establishing extraterritorial privileges and fixed low tariffs that would shape Sanese-Dagiti relations for decades.

=== Great Adonian War ===
{{main|Great Adonian War}}

During the Great Adonian War (1939–1945), Dagit aligned itself with the [[Fosian Powers]], leveraging its strategic position in the Shendan Ocean to provide critical logistical support for naval operations. The port facilities at Akyatan served as essential resupply points for Fosian fleets. The war brought both hardship and opportunity—the disruption of trade routes caused economic contraction, but the military buildup stimulated domestic manufacturing capabilities.

=== Post-war development ===

The immediate post-war years were defined by reconstruction and the rise of a "new merchant oligarchy"—wealthy families who parlayed control of import licenses, shipping contracts, and government connections into vast fortunes. The Maceda, Gonzaga, Padilla, and Yanson families dominated the post-war economy, cultivating close relationships with the political establishment.

The election of President [[Ferdinand Maceda]] in 1965 marked the consolidation of merchant oligarchy rule. His administration presided over rapid economic growth, but wealth remained concentrated in the hands of the elite while the gap between rich and poor widened dramatically.

=== The 1994 coup attempt and the 2009 Dagiti Revolution ===

By the early 1990s, the contradictions of the post-war order produced a crisis of legitimacy. On 15 August 1994, a faction of junior officers led by Colonel [[Antonio Silvosa]] launched a coup attempt, citing endemic corruption. Though the coup failed, the Silvosa faction became heroes to a public disillusioned with the ruling elite.

The 1998 election of President [[Ramon Magalona]], a former movie star, initially promised change but delivered unprecedented corruption. The ''[[Bulkan Pondo Scandal]]'' of 2006 triggered mass protests that culminated in the '''Dagiti Revolution of 2009'''. On 15 October 2009, President Magalona resigned and fled into exile. His son, [[Enrique Magalona]], was elected president in 2023, marking the restoration of the Magalona political brand.

== Geography ==
[[File:Akyatan Aerial View.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Aerial view of Akyatan's central business district and the Dagiti River estuary.]]

Akyatan is situated on the eastern coast of Kabisayaan, the main island of the Dagit archipelago. The city faces the Shendan Ocean to the east and is built around the estuarine mouth of the '''Dagiti River'''. The strategic location forms a natural deep-water harbor that has made the city a crucial maritime hub for centuries.

The city occupies a flat alluvial plain, largely reclaimed from mangrove swamps due to urbanization from the colonial period to the present. The '''Dagiti River''' traverses the city center, separating the historic Ciudad Vieja district from the modern business districts. Because Dagit sits squarely within the primary path of the Eastern Typhoons, Akyatan's architectural development is heavily adapted to withstand extreme cyclical flooding and high-velocity winds.

=== Climate ===
Akyatan has a [[tropical monsoon climate]] (Köppen: ''Am''), characterized by high temperatures year-round with distinct wet and dry seasons. The wet season typically runs from May through December, with peak typhoon activity between August and November.

