Each district maintains its own town center equipped with a plaza, municipal building, fire station, police station, and public market. Ciudad Vieja serves as the historic and cultural heart of the city, while Puerto Lacunya functions as the primary commercial and financial center.

== Demographics ==
As of the 2024 census, Akyatan has a population of 3,847,215, making it the largest city in Dagit. The metropolitan area has a population of approximately 5.9 million. The city's population is characterized by ethnic and linguistic diversity, with significant communities of Volisanian-Dagiti ''mestizos'', Zongese-Dagiti, and migrants from other parts of Dagit.

Both [[Dagiti language|Dagiti]] and [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] are widely spoken, reflecting the nation's bilingual heritage developed through centuries of commercial interaction rather than colonial conquest.

=== Religion ===
The religious landscape reflects Dagit's syncretic spiritual heritage. The majority practices a localized form of Volisanian religion intertwined with [[Anitism]], the indigenous belief system. Many residents maintain household shrines to ''anito'' (ancestral spirits) and ''diwata'' (nature spirits), guided historically by female spiritual leaders known as ''[[babaylan]]''.

== Economy ==
[[File:Akyatan CBD.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The [[Puerto Lacunya]] district serves as Akyatan's primary financial and commercial center.]]
Akyatan serves as the economic heart of Dagit, generating approximately ε892.4 billion in [[Gross Domestic Product|GDP]] as of 2024—approximately 32% of the nation's total economic output despite containing only about 3% of the national population. The city's economy is highly diversified, with significant contributions from financial services, information technology, manufacturing, tourism, logistics, and [[business process outsourcing]]. Akyatan is classified as a "Beta+" [[global city]] by the Adonian Globalization and World Cities Research Network, reflecting its established links to the world economic system.

=== Financial services ===
As the nation's capital and primary financial center, Akyatan serves as the headquarters for most of Dagit's major corporations, banks, and financial institutions. The [[Akyatan Stock Exchange]] (ASE), established in 1952, is the largest securities exchange in the Shendan Ocean region by market capitalization, with over 380 listed companies and a total market capitalization exceeding ε4.2 trillion as of 2024. The exchange's main trading floor is located in the historic [[Magalona Tower]], while its electronic trading operations are housed in the modern [[Treñas Financial Center]] in the Puerto Lacunya district.

The [[Central Bank of Dagit]] and the [[Ministry of Finance (Dagit)|Ministry of Finance]] are both headquartered in the [[San Esteban]] district, forming the core of the city's "Financial Mile" along Mabini Avenue. The city hosts 42 commercial banks, including the headquarters of the "Big Three" Dagiti banks: the [[Dagiti Development Bank]], [[Akyatan Banking Corporation]], and [[Shendan Banking and Trust]]. Combined, these institutions hold approximately 68% of all banking assets in Dagit.

The [[Shendan Development Bank]], a multinational financial institution owned jointly by Dagit, [[Okaiken]], [[Yanbaru]], and the [[Kingdom of Zong]], maintains its headquarters in the [[Dagumnan]] district. The bank provides development financing for infrastructure projects throughout the Shendan Ocean region, with total assets exceeding ε180 billion.

=== Information technology and business process outsourcing ===
{{See also|Information technology in Dagit}}
The [[information technology]] and [[business process outsourcing]] (BPO) sector has emerged as a major driver of Akyatan's economic growth since the early 2000s. The city is now the third-largest BPO hub in western Fosia, after [[Okami]] and [[Merced]], employing approximately 187,000 workers in over 320 companies as of 2024. The sector generated ε127.5 billion in revenue in 2024, accounting for approximately 14% of the city's GDP.

The growth of the BPO industry has been driven by the city's highly educated, English-speaking workforce, competitive labor costs, and modern telecommunications infrastructure. The [[Akyatan IT Park]] in the Puerto Lacunya district and the [[Kabisayaan Cyberpark]] in Sampaguita serve as the primary hubs for technology companies, housing major operations for multinational firms including customer support centers, software development facilities, and back-office processing units.

[[File:Akyatan IT Park.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The [[Akyatan IT Park]] in the Puerto Lacunya district employs over 45,000 IT-BPO workers.]]
Beyond traditional call centers, Akyatan has developed capabilities in higher-value services including healthcare information management, financial analytics, software development, and animation. The city's animation studios have contributed to major regional film and television productions, while several software development firms have established partnerships with technology companies in [[Okaiken]] and [[Volisania]]. The [[Dagiti Game Development Association]], headquartered in the IT Park, represents 47 game development studios employing approximately 8,500 workers.

=== Trade and logistics ===
The city's port facilities handle approximately 65% of Dagit's international trade by volume, serving as a crucial [[transshipment hub]] for goods moving between Fosia, Illypnia, and Lurandia. The [[Akyatan International Container Terminal]] is one of the busiest ports in the western Shendan Ocean, processing over 12.4 million [[twenty-foot equivalent unit]]s (TEU) in 2024—a 7.2% increase from the previous year. The port's strategic location along major shipping lanes has made it a preferred hub for container transshipment, with approximately 40% of throughput consisting of cargo bound for other destinations.

The [[Akyatan Free Port Authority]], established in 1992, operates a [[special economic zone]] adjacent to the main port facilities. The zone offers tax incentives and streamlined customs procedures to export-oriented businesses, housing over 180 companies engaged in manufacturing, warehousing, and distribution activities. Major logistics operators including [[Maersk Dagit]], [[Shendan Logistics Corporation]], and [[Fosian Express]] maintain regional distribution centers within the zone.

=== Manufacturing ===
Manufacturing remains an important sector, contributing approximately 18% of the city's GDP. The [[Sampaguita]] district houses [[export processing zone]]s specializing in electronics, garments, food processing, and light manufacturing. The city's industrial base has gradually shifted toward higher-value manufacturing and technology since the 2010s, with several multinational semiconductor and electronics companies establishing production facilities.

The electronics sector dominates manufacturing output, with major facilities operated by [[Shendan Semiconductor]] (employing 12,000 workers), [[Dagiti Electronics Corporation]], and [[Fosian Microelectronics]]. These facilities produce integrated circuits, consumer electronics components, and precision instruments for export to markets across Fosia and Illypnia. The garment industry, concentrated in smaller workshops throughout the Sampaguita district, employs approximately 35,000 workers producing clothing for export to North Fosian and Illypnian markets.

Food processing represents a growing subsector, with numerous facilities processing local agricultural products including coconut oil, tropical fruits, and seafood for domestic consumption and export. The [[Akyatan Fish Port Complex]] in the Dagumnan district is one of the largest fish landing and processing facilities in the Shendan Ocean region, handling approximately 280,000 metric tons of seafood annually.

=== Tourism ===
{{Main|Tourism in Akyatan}}
Tourism contributes significantly to the local economy, with the preserved [[Cinc Parets Heritage Zone]] in Ciudad Vieja attracting heritage tourists from across Adonia. In 2024, the city received approximately 4.8 million domestic tourists and 1.2 million international visitors, generating an estimated ε42.3 billion in tourism revenue—a 12% increase from 2023 figures.

[[File:Cinc Parets Night.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The historic [[Cinc Parets Heritage Zone]] illuminated during the annual ''Fiesta sang Luz'' festival.]]
The city's vibrant street festivals, particularly the annual [[Kabisayaan Festival]] held each January, draw hundreds of thousands of visitors. The festival alone generates approximately ε3.8 billion in economic activity through hotel bookings, restaurant sales, and retail purchases. The city's convention and meetings industry has also grown significantly, with the [[Akyatan Convention Center]] hosting over 200 events annually including international conferences, trade shows, and corporate meetings.

Hotel occupancy rates averaged 78% in 2024, with higher rates during peak festival seasons. The city's hospitality sector includes over 180 hotels and resorts, ranging from luxury international chains to boutique heritage properties within the Cinc Parets Heritage Zone. The [[Magalona Heritage Hotel]], housed in a restored 18th-century colonial mansion, has been recognized as one of the premier heritage hotels in western Fosia.

=== Real estate ===
The real estate sector has experienced sustained growth driven by demand for commercial office space, residential condominiums, and industrial facilities. Major mixed-use developments in the Puerto Lacunya district have transformed former industrial areas into modern urban centers combining office towers, residential buildings, retail complexes, and public spaces.

The [[Treñas Holdings]] group has been particularly active in developing large-scale projects including the [[Shendan Tower]] (68 stories, the tallest building in Dagit), [[Magalona City Center]] (a 25-hectare mixed-use development), and the [[Harborfront Complex]] (luxury residential towers along the Dagiti River estuary). Foreign investors, particularly from [[Okaiken]], [[Volisania]], and [[Zong]], have participated in joint ventures for major developments.

