Line 1, the system's oldest line, connects the historic Cinc Parets Heritage Zone with the modern financial center of Puerto Lacunya, running along the historic [[Mabini Avenue]] corridor. Line 2 provides a critical connection between the port facilities in Dagumnan and the residential districts. Line 3 serves the government center in San Esteban and the industrial zones of Sampaguita. Line 4 connects the heritage Ciudad Vieja district with the modern Isla Verde development.

Line 5, the longest line in the system, extends northward to serve both established neighborhoods and new suburban developments. Line 6, the Airport Express, provides premium rapid service between Akyatan International Airport and the city center with a travel time of just 18 minutes. Line 7, the newest addition, forms a circumferential loop connecting all radial lines and providing circumferential connectivity around the urban core.

The system operates from 5:00 AM to midnight daily, with extended hours during major festivals. All lines use a unified [[tap-and-go]] payment system integrated with bus services through the [[Akyatan Transit Card]]. The system is undergoing a major expansion program, with planned extensions to Line 3 and a new Line 8 currently under construction.

==== Bus services ====
Akyatan's bus network is operated by two competing private companies under franchise agreements with the city government. The system comprises approximately 2,400 buses serving 85 routes across the metropolitan area, carrying approximately 1.6 million passengers daily.

[[Akyatan Metrobus Corporation]] (AMC), a subsidiary of the [[Magalona Group]], operates the larger network with 1,400 buses serving 52 routes. The company operates a fleet of modern [[articulated bus]]es and [[double-decker bus]]es on major corridors, including the high-traffic [[Circumferential Route C-1]] and the [[Radial Routes R-1 through R-12]] extending from the city center to suburban municipalities. AMC introduced the city's first [[electric bus]] fleet in 2022 and has committed to converting 40% of its fleet to electric vehicles by 2030.

[[Shendan Transit Corporation]] (STC), owned by a consortium including [[Treñas Holdings]] and investors from [[Okaiken]], operates 1,000 buses on 33 routes. The company focuses on premium express services and suburban feeder routes, operating distinctive orange-and-white buses equipped with [[air conditioning]], [[free WiFi]], and [[USB charging ports]]. STC's flagship [[Airport Express]] service provides direct connections from suburban areas to Akyatan International Airport, competing with Metro Line 6 for airport travelers.

Both companies participate in the integrated [[Akyatan Transit Card]] system, allowing seamless transfers between buses and the Metro system. The city government regulates fares, service frequencies, and route coverage through the [[Akyatan Transportation Regulatory Board]]. Competition between the two operators has been credited with improving service quality and encouraging innovation, though critics argue that the fragmented system creates inconsistencies in service standards across routes.

==== Other road transport ====
[[Jeepneys]] (shared taxis converted from military surplus vehicles) remain popular for short-distance travel, particularly in areas not well-served by the Metro or bus networks. Approximately 12,000 jeepneys operate in the metropolitan area, serving fixed routes with flexible stops. The iconic vehicles, decorated with elaborate artwork and Christian imagery, have become symbols of Dagiti urban culture. However, the city government has implemented a modernization program requiring older vehicles to be replaced with cleaner, safer alternatives by 2028.

[[Tricycle]]s (motorcycle taxis with sidecars) serve residential neighborhoods and provide last-mile connectivity to transit stations. The city issues approximately 45,000 tricycle permits, though enforcement of regulations has been inconsistent. [[Ride-hailing services]] including [[Grab Dagit]] and [[GoCar]] have grown rapidly since 2015, providing an alternative to traditional taxis and public transit.

The city is connected to the national highway network via the [[Kabisayaan Expressway]], a 142-kilometer limited-access toll road providing high-speed connections to the interior provinces. The [[Akyatan Circumferential Road]] (a 45-kilometer ring road) facilitates traffic movement around the urban core, while the [[Harbor Boulevard]] coastal highway connects the port facilities with the central business district. The city's [[smart traffic management system]], implemented in 2019, uses [[artificial intelligence]] to optimize traffic signal timing and has reduced average commute times by an estimated 12%.

=== Active transportation ===
Akyatan has invested in active transportation infrastructure as part of its sustainability initiatives. The city maintains a network of [[protected bicycle lanes]] spanning 85 kilometers, with plans to expand to 150 kilometers by 2030. The [[Akyatan Bike Share]] program, launched in 2018, operates 3,500 bicycles at 350 stations throughout the metropolitan area, recording over 2.4 million trips in 2024.

The [[Dagiti River Esplanade]], a 12-kilometer pedestrian promenade along the Dagiti River, provides a scenic route for walking and cycling through the city center. The [[Cinc Parets Pedestrian Zone]] in the historic district restricts vehicle access during daytime hours, creating a pedestrian-friendly environment for tourists and residents.

== Culture ==
{{Main|Culture of Dagit}}
[[File:Kabisayaan Festival.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The [[Kabisayaan Festival]], held annually in January, is one of the largest street festivals in the Shendan Ocean region.]]
Akyatan's culture reflects its rich history as a crossroads of Fosian, Illypnian, and indigenous Dagiti influences. The city's cultural landscape is characterized by the synthesis of traditional [[Anitism|Anitist]] practices with Volisanian colonial heritage and modern global influences, creating a distinctively syncretic urban culture. In 2019, Akyatan was designated as a [[City of Gastronomy|Creative City of Gastronomy]], recognizing its unique culinary traditions and commitment to preserving indigenous food heritage.

=== Festivals ===
The city is renowned for its vibrant [[street festival]]s, which evolved from pre-colonial harvest and fishing rituals into massive public celebrations that regularly shut down urban centers. These festivals represent a syncretic blend of indigenous Anitist spirituality, Volisanian Catholic religious traditions, and modern civic pride.

The most significant of these is the [[Kabisayaan Festival]], held each January to commemorate the Dagiti Revolution and honor the ''[[Santo Niño sang Kabisayaan]]'' (Holy Child of Kabisayaan). The festival features elaborate [[Ati-Atihan]]-style street dancing, where participants paint their bodies in elaborate tribal patterns and dance through the streets to the rhythm of indigenous drums and modern brass bands. The festival draws approximately 2.5 million spectators over its nine-day duration, making it one of the largest street festivals in the Shendan Ocean region. The festival's famous chant—"''Hala Bira! Akyat Akyat!''" ({{lang-dtp|Let go! Climb climb!}})—references both the revolutionary storming of Cinc Parets and the spiritual ascent toward divine grace.

The [[Manggahan Festival]], held each May in the Kawayanan district, celebrates the mango harvest and the city's agricultural heritage. The festival features street dancing with performers dressed in bright yellow and green costumes representing mango trees and fruit, along with agricultural fairs, mango-eating contests, and culinary competitions showcasing the region's famous ''Carabao'' mango variety. The festival attracts approximately 800,000 visitors annually and generates significant economic activity for the agricultural communities surrounding the city.

Other major festivals include the ''[[Fiesta sang Luz]]'' ({{lang-dtp|Festival of Light}}) in December, which illuminates the Cinc Parets Heritage Zone with elaborate lantern displays and fireworks; the ''[[Parada sang mga Bapor]]'' ({{lang-dtp|Parade of Ships}}) in August, a maritime procession honoring ancestral sea spirits; and the ''[[Pista sang Anito]]'' in November, a more intimate observance focused on ancestral veneration and spiritual practices.

=== Heritage architecture ===
{{See also|List of cultural properties of Dagit in Akyatan}}
Akyatan possesses one of the finest collections of colonial architecture in western Fosia, centered on the [[Cinc Parets Heritage Zone]] which was designated a World Heritage Site in 1987. The district contains over 200 preserved structures dating from the 17th to 19th centuries, including religious monuments, government buildings, and merchant houses representing a fusion of Volisanian Baroque, indigenous Dagiti, and Sanese architectural influences.

[[File:Cinc Parets Cathedral.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The [[Cathedral of Saint James the Apostle (Akyatan)|Cathedral of Saint James the Apostle]], completed in 1786, exemplifies Volisanian Baroque architecture adapted to tropical conditions.]]
The [[Cathedral of Saint James the Apostle (Akyatan)|Cathedral of Saint James the Apostle]], completed in 1786, serves as the seat of the [[Archdiocese of Akyatan]] and represents one of the finest examples of Volisanian Baroque architecture in Fosia. The cathedral's façade features intricate stone carvings incorporating indigenous botanical motifs alongside traditional Christian iconography, while its interior houses a renowned collection of 18th-century [[retablo]]s (altar pieces) and a historic pipe organ imported from Volisania in 1842.

The [[Magalona ancestral houses]] in the Ciudad Vieja district represent a distinctive architectural style known as ''Bahay na Bato'' ({{lang-dtp|House of Stone}}), developed during the colonial period to withstand earthquakes and typhoons. These two-story structures feature ground floors of volcanic stone or coral blocks and upper floors of hardwood, with wide capiz-shell windows that provide ventilation while filtering the harsh tropical sunlight. The district contains over 60 preserved ancestral houses, many still occupied by descendants of the original colonial-era families.

The [[Liko-Liko Caves]] on the outskirts of the city contain significant archaeological sites with evidence of human habitation dating to approximately 2000 BCE. The caves house ancient [[jar burial]]s, petroglyphs, and artifacts related to early Anitist religious practices, and have been designated a [[National Cultural Treasure (Dagit)|National Cultural Treasure]].

=== Visual and performing arts ===
Akyatan has a thriving arts scene that blends traditional craftsmanship with contemporary expression. The city is particularly renowned for its textile traditions, especially ''[[Yinbata]]'' ({{lang-dtp|resist dyeing}}) which creates intricate patterns on fabrics using indigenous dyes derived from local plants, minerals, and marine sources. The [[Akyatan Weaver's Cooperative]], established in 1952, continues to preserve and promote these traditional techniques, with member artisans producing fabrics for both ceremonial use and commercial export.

The city's contemporary art scene centers on the [[Akyatan Contemporary Art Center]] in the Puerto Lacunya district, which hosts rotating exhibitions featuring both established and emerging artists from across the Shendan Ocean region. The center's collection includes significant works by Dagiti modernists as well as contemporary pieces exploring themes of postcolonial identity, urbanization, and environmental change.

Traditional performing arts include the ''[[Komedya sang Kabisayaan]]'', a form of musical theater depicting conflicts between Christian and Moorish kingdoms that evolved from Volisanian theatrical traditions. The [[Dagiti Cultural Foundation]] maintains a professional komedya troupe that performs throughout the archipelago and has adapted the traditional form to address contemporary social issues.

=== Music ===
{{See also|Music of Dagit}}
Akyatan's musical traditions encompass both indigenous forms and modern popular music. Traditional Dagiti music is characterized by its use of the ''[[kulintang]]'' (gong ensemble), indigenous bamboo instruments, and vocal styles featuring complex melismatic ornamentation. The [[Shendan Conservatory of Music]], affiliated with the University of Akyatan, maintains an active program of research and performance of traditional music.

The city has emerged as a major center for popular music in the Shendan Ocean region, particularly through the development of [[Island Groove]]—a genre that fuses traditional Dagiti musical elements with reggae, acoustic rock, and hip-hop. The genre was pioneered by artists including [[CHICO CARLITO]], whose 2015 album ''"Mga Sugilanon sang Isla"'' ({{lang-dtp|Stories of the Island}}) achieved commercial success across the archipelago and helped mainstream the use of Dagiti language in popular music.

The city hosts several major music festivals, including the annual ''[[Tunog sang Dagat]]'' ({{lang-dtp|Sound of the Sea}}) folk festival in March and the ''[[Akyatan Jazz Festival]]'' in September, which attracts performers from across Fosia and Illypnia.

=== Cuisine ===
{{Main|Dagiti cuisine}}
[[File:La Paz Batchoy.jpg|thumb|left|250px|''[[La Paz Batchoy]]'', a noodle soup invented in the San Esteban district in the 1930s, has become Akyatan's most famous culinary export.]]
Akyatan is considered the culinary capital of Dagit, with a distinctive cuisine that combines indigenous Dagiti ingredients with Volisanian, Sanese, and Zongese influences. The city's designation as a [[UNESCO Creative City of Gastronomy]] in 2019 recognized both its rich culinary heritage and its contemporary food innovation. The city's [[hawker center]]s, [[carinderia]]s (small neighborhood eateries), and [[night market]]s serve dishes characterized by bold, sour-savory flavor profiles utilizing heavy amounts of [[vinegar]], indigenous [[citrus]] (particularly ''[[calamansi]]''), and [[fermentation|fermented seafood]].

Signature dishes include ''[[kare-kare]]'' (oxtail stew in peanut sauce), ''[[sinigang]]'' (sour tamarind soup), ''[[lechon]]'' (whole roasted pig), and ''[[kinilaw]]'' (raw fish cured in coconut vinegar, ginger, and chilies). The city's ''[[La Paz Batchoy]]'', a noodle soup invented in the [[San Esteban]] district in the 1930s by Chinese-Dagiti immigrant Federico Guillergan, has become nationally famous and is served in restaurants throughout Dagit. The dish features pork innards, liver, and crushed pork cracklings in a rich broth poured over miki noodles, topped with raw egg that cooks in the hot broth.

[[File:Akyatan Night Market.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The [[Ciudad Vieja Night Market]] serves over 15,000 customers nightly with traditional and contemporary Dagiti street food.]]
The city's seafood cuisine reflects its maritime heritage, with the [[Akyatan Fish Port Complex]] supplying fresh catches daily to restaurants throughout the city. Popular seafood dishes include ''[[inihaw na bangus]]'' (grilled milkfish), ''[[sinigang na hipon]]'' (sour shrimp soup), and ''[[tuna panga]]'' (grilled tuna jaw). The coastal Dagumnan district is known for its seafood restaurants specializing in dishes featuring the region's abundant tuna, mackerel, and shellfish.

Traditional sweets and desserts include ''[[barquillos]]'' (thin rolled wafer cookies), ''[[pinasugbo]]'' (caramelized banana chips), and ''[[biscocho]]'' (twice-baked bread). The city is also famous for its [[mango]] products, particularly from the Kawayanan district, which produces the renowned ''Carabao'' mango variety exported throughout the region.

=== Sports ===
[[Basketball]] is the most popular sport in Akyatan, with the city hosting the [[Akyatan Royals]] professional team in the [[Dagit Basketball Association]] (DBA). The team has won 14 DBA championships since the league's founding in 1985, the most of any franchise. The city has produced numerous players who have achieved success in regional and international leagues, particularly in [[Okaiken]] and [[Volisania]].

[[Football (soccer)|Football]] has grown in popularity since the 2000s, with the [[Akyatan City F.C.]] competing in the [[Shendan Ocean Premier League]]. The [[Akyatan Sports Complex]], constructed in 2019 for the [[Shendan Games]], includes a 25,000-seat stadium that hosts both football matches and major concerts.

Traditional indigenous sports, including [[arnis]] (stick fighting), ''[[sipa]]'' (kick volleyball), and ''[[sabong]]'' (cockfighting), remain popular recreational activities. The city hosts the annual [[Akyatan Indigenous Games]] each October, featuring competitions in traditional martial arts, maritime skills including [[outrigger canoe]] racing, and indigenous games. The [[Dagiti Arnis Federation]], headquartered in Akyatan, governs the sport nationally and has developed standardized competition rules that have been adopted throughout the Shendan Ocean region.

[[Boxing]] enjoys a strong following, with the city producing several national and regional champions. The [[Akyatan Boxing Gym]] in the Sampaguita district has trained multiple title holders and hosts professional boxing events throughout the year. [[E-sports]] has emerged as a growing spectator activity, with the [[Akyatan Cyber Arena]] hosting major tournaments and attracting significant online viewership.

=== Media ===
Akyatan serves as the media capital of Dagit, hosting the headquarters of the major national television networks, newspapers, and digital media companies. The city's media landscape reflects the nation's bilingual character, with content produced in both Dagiti and Volisanian.

Major television networks include the government-owned [[Dagiti Broadcasting Corporation]] (DBC), [[Volisanian Television Dagit]] (VTD), and the private [[Akyatan Broadcasting System]] (ABS). These networks operate multiple channels offering news, entertainment, sports, and educational programming. The city also hosts regional bureaus for international networks including [[Shendan News Network]] and [[Fosia Today]].

Leading newspapers include ''[[The Akyatan Chronicle]]'' (the nation's oldest Mercian-language newspaper, founded 1886), ''[[Dagiti Daily]]'' (the largest Dagiti-language daily), and ''[[Kabisayaan Times]]'' (a bilingual broadsheet). The city's tabloid press, including the popular ''[[Akyatan Star]]'' and ''[[NewsWatch Dagit]]'', provides extensive coverage of entertainment, sports, and crime stories.

The city's film industry, centered in the [[Puerto Lacunya]] district, produces the majority of Dagiti-language films distributed throughout the archipelago. The [[Dagiti Film Development Board]] provides funding and support for local productions, while the annual [[Akyatan International Film Festival]] in November showcases works from across the Shendan Ocean region and beyond.

=== Religious sites ===
Akyatan is a significant center of religious tourism, drawing pilgrims and heritage tourists to its numerous historic churches, shrines, and spiritual sites. The dominant faith is [[Volisanian Catholicism]], a syncretic form of Christianity that incorporates elements of indigenous Anitist practice, but the city also houses significant communities of traditional Anitists, Buddhists, and practitioners of other faiths.

[[File:Shrine of Our Lady.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The [[Shrine of Our Lady of the Candles]] attracts over 2 million pilgrims annually.]]
The [[Shrine of Our Lady of the Candles]] in the Ciudad Vieja district is the most important Marian shrine in Dagit, attracting over 2 million pilgrims annually for the feast day on February 2. The shrine houses a venerated statue of the Virgin Mary that local tradition holds was discovered floating in Akyatan Bay by indigenous fishermen in 1585.

The [[San Agustin Church (Akyatan)|San Agustin Church]] in the Cinc Parets Heritage Zone, completed in 1704, is the oldest stone church in Dagit and one of the few colonial structures to survive both the 1824 earthquake and the 1850 revolution largely intact. The church's museum houses an extensive collection of colonial-era religious art, including ivory santos (saint statues), liturgical vestments, and colonial-era documents.

For practitioners of traditional [[Anitism]], the [[Liko-Liko Caves]] on the outskirts of the city serve as an important pilgrimage site. The caves contain ancient shrines to the ''[[diwata]]'' (nature spirits) and are the site of annual rituals during the ''Pista sang Anito'' in November. The [[Dagiti Anitist Association]] maintains a temple and cultural center adjacent to the caves, offering educational programs about indigenous spiritual practices.

The city also houses [[Buddhist temples]] serving the significant Sanese and Zongese communities, including the historic [[Shendan Temple]] (founded 1756) and the modern [[Kabisayaan Buddhist Center]]. The [[Grand Mosque of Akyatan]] serves the Muslim community, which includes both indigenous converts and immigrants from mainland Fosia.

== Education ==
[[File:University of Akyatan.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The main building of the [[University of Akyatan]], founded in 1867.]]
Akyatan serves as the educational center of Dagit. The '''University of Akyatan''', founded in 1867, is the oldest and largest university in the country. Literacy rates exceed 98%.

The city is also home to several specialized research institutions, including the [[Shendan Ocean Marine Research Institute]] and the [[Dagiti National Museum]]. The [[National Library of Dagit]], located adjacent to the University of Akyatan campus, houses the largest collection of Dagiti and Volisanian manuscripts in the nation.

Primary and secondary education is administered by the [[Akyatan City Schools Division]] under the national [[Ministry of Education (Dagit)|Ministry of Education]]. The city maintains an extensive network of public elementary and secondary schools, supplemented by private institutions operated by religious organizations and educational foundations.

